 "name": "Urban Nature for Older Adults: Sensory Dimensions, Preference and Restoration",
 "description": "Faithful replication with the paper's own stimuli: the eight photographs from Figure 1b of Qiu, Chen & Gao (2021), Int. J. Environ. Res. Public Health 18(2): 509 (CC BY 4.0), two for each of four urban natural environments in Baoji Botanical Garden: open green space, partly-closed green space, closed green space and blue space. Each participant is randomly assigned to one environment, sees its two photographs, and rates the eight perceived sensory dimensions (serene, nature, rich in species, space, prospect, refuge, social, culture; 1–5, with the paper's Table 1 descriptions), preference (1–5, with an optional reason) and the 16-item Perceived Restorativeness Scale (1–5). The paper found blue space was preferred most (4.40) and closed green space least (3.63); its means are embedded in the page for comparison. Differences from the original study: (1) online photographs instead of printed photos shown in the field; (2) the age question also offers under 60, while the paper recruited adults aged 60 or over; (3) the paper does not print its PRS items, so the 16-item English wording is taken from Frontiers in Public Health 10:801453 (Table 5); (4) the photographs are small crops of the published figure.",
